      "problem": "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =(0)+(-1)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(0)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(1)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=3$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3 & ≈-(3-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(3-\\pi )^{3} \\\\  & =0.141\\,12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places.",
