    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-83201",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series",
      "problem": "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(x-\\pi )^{4}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +1$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1) & ≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =-0.542,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places.",
