      "problem": "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡4.$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=4$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4 & ≈-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(4-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(4-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(4-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-0.654\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places.",
